WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.110
Earth zooms around the sun at 110,000 kph,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.330
but what if it just...stopped?

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.126
Well, for one thing, you'd
have only a month to live.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.600
First off, if Earth stopped short,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.083
you'd actually die immediately

00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:24.860
because you'd fly off the planet

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.710
and hurtle into space,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.970
just like you lurch forward in a car

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.760
when a driver slams on the brakes.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.690
But if Earth slows down more gradually,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:39.090
you'd instead suffer a slower
(but still horrific) death.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.243
That's because the planet is about to get

00:00:41.243 --> 00:00:43.820
very, very hot.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.589
You see, the sun's tremendous gravity

00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:48.800
pulls the Earth right toward it.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.050
Meanwhile, the planet
is trying to whiz away

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.790
in this direction.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.540
This tug of war keeps us in orbit.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.480
But if the Earth slows down and stops,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.120
the sun wins and yanks us toward our doom.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.350
In fact, a model from the
University of Colorado

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.620
shows us how long that
terrible trip would take.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.950
Just two months.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.130
In the first week, you wouldn't notice

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.670
much of a change.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.020
The average global temperature would climb

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.350
less than 1 degree Celsius

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.240
and hover around 16
degrees for another week.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.920
But the closer we get, the
stronger the sun's pull

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.930
and the faster we go.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.246
This causes the temperature
to rise exponentially.

00:01:35.246 --> 00:01:38.637
By day 21, global
temperatures will have jumped

00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:40.628
to 35 degrees.

00:01:40.628 --> 00:01:44.050
That's hotter than an
average day in the Sahara.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.030
And with desert heat
comes desert droughts,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.260
sparking wildfires and killing crops.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.674
UV radiation from the sun is so strong,

00:01:53.674 --> 00:01:58.080
we'd get severe sunburn after
just 15 minutes outdoors.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.932
At this point, our
bodies also will have hit

00:02:00.932 --> 00:02:03.440
the upper limit of their heat tolerance.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.150
Any hotter, and heat stroke can set in,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.240
leading to exhaustion,
delirium, or even a coma.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.542
And guess what? It gets hotter.

00:02:13.542 --> 00:02:18.518
By day 35, world temperatures
will reach 48 degrees.

00:02:18.518 --> 00:02:22.300
That's as hot as an average
summer day in Death Valley,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.810
one of the hottest places on Earth.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.730
Most mammals, birds, and insects die

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.750
as the proteins that make up their cells

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.599
start to cook.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.760
Yes, even roaches can't
withstand this apocalypse.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.320
If we want to live,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.970
we need some indoor
air-conditioning, stat.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.860
Except, the amount of energy you need

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.920
to run millions of AC units at once

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.170
would wipe out power
grids around the world.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.743
So, with no way to keep
cool, humanity dies.

00:02:54.743 --> 00:02:59.150
By day 41, the Earth has passed by Venus.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.845
We're now the second planet from the sun,

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:05.380
and temperatures have
climbed to 66 degrees.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.840
But believe it or not,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.270
some creatures still cling to life.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.420
Like Thermus aquaticus,
a bacteria that lives

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.740
in Yellowstone's hot geysers.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.700
But less than a week later,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.490
temperatures are hot enough to boil water

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.250
and kill even that bacteria.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.870
By day 54, temperatures
surpass 160 degrees,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.170
and the last remnant of
life on Earth flickers out.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.230
Soon after, the barren planet
crosses Mercury's orbit.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.492
For its last week of existence,

00:03:37.492 --> 00:03:40.550
Earth is the first planet from the sun.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.600
Until day 65. The final day.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.632
By now, the sun's extreme gravity

00:03:47.632 --> 00:03:50.370
stretches the planet into an oval shape,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.650
and magma begins to
leak through the crust.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.900
At about noon, Earth tears apart,

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.410
bleeding liquid rock as temperatures hit

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.243
3,800 degrees.

00:04:02.243 --> 00:04:04.959
Our planet is no more.

